WEBVTT

00:00:01.010 --> 00:00:38.000
<v Ira Glass>We think of our lives as a series of events, of things happening one after another. But, you know, it's just as accurate to see our lives as a series of things that don't happen to us. There's this story where all the crucial action takes place within just a short bike ride from the spot where I speak to you from right now, here in Chicago. The setting, a summer, much like the one we're living through even as I speak, on this humid, midwestern day. It's a piece of fiction, a really, really great piece of fiction actually, by a writer named Stuart Dybek. We Didn't.
<v Ira Glass>我们认为生活是一系列事件的发生，一件接一件。但你知道吗，把生活看作是一系列没有发生在我们身上的事情，也同样准确。有这样一个故事，所有关键情节都发生在我现在说话的地方——芝加哥，离这儿不远的一次短途骑行范围内。背景是一个夏天，就像我们此刻正在经历的这个，在这个潮湿的中西部日子里。这是一部虚构作品，实际上是一位名叫斯图尔特·戴贝克（Stuart Dybek）的作家写得非常、非常棒的虚构作品。题目是《我们没做》。

00:00:38.000 --> 00:01:14.180
<v Ira Glass>We didn't in the light. We didn't in darkness. We didn't in the fresh-cut summer grass, or in the mounds of autumn leaves, or in the snow, where moonlight threw down our shadows. We didn't in your room on the canopy bed you slept in, the bed you'd slept in as a child. Or in the backseat of my father's rusted Rambler, which smelled of the smoked chubs and kielbasa that he delivered on weekends for my Uncle Vincent's meat market. We didn't in your mother's Buick 8, where a rosary twined the rear view mirror like a beaded, black snake with silver, cruciform fangs.
<v Ira Glass>我们没在光天化日之下做。我们没在黑暗中做。我们没在那修剪整齐的夏日草坪上做，也没在那秋叶堆中做，或者在雪地里做，那里的月光投射出我们的影子。我们没在你的房间里，在那张你睡过的顶篷床上做，那张你小时候睡过的床。也没在父亲那辆生锈的漫步者（Rambler）汽车后座做，那车里总有一股烟熏红点鲑和波兰香肠的味道，那是他周末为文森特叔叔的肉铺送货留下的。我们没在你母亲的别克8里做，那里的后视镜上缠着一串念珠，像一条带着银色十字架毒牙的黑色珠子蛇。

00:01:14.180 --> 00:01:58.150
<v Ira Glass>After this opening paragraph, Stuart Dybek has 11 pages, thousands of words, describing all of the things that two people can do when they're not doing something. Turns out, that not doing something is every bit as vivid and complicated an experience as doing something. But, my friend, where are the stories of the things that we do when we're not doing the thing itself? Well, we bring those to you today, right here. Part of our ongoing quest to document our actual lives in this country. From WBEZ Chicago and Public Radio International, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass.
<v Ira Glass>这段开场白之后，斯图尔特·戴贝克用11页纸、数千字描述了两个人不做某事时能做的所有事情。事实证明，不做某事和做某事一样，都是生动而复杂的经历。但是，朋友，那些我们不做某事时所做的事情的故事在哪里呢？好吧，我们今天就把它们带给你，就在这里。这是我们记录在这个国家真实生活持续探索的一部分。来自芝加哥WBEZ电台和国际公共广播电台（PRI），这里是《美国生活》（This American Life）。我是艾拉·格拉斯（Ira Glass）。

00:01:58.150 --> 00:02:03.930
<v Ira Glass>Each week on our program, of course, we choose a theme. Today's show, We Didn't.
<v Ira Glass>当然，我们每周的节目都会选择一个主题。今天的节目是《我们没做》。

00:02:03.930 --> 00:02:11.830
<v Ira Glass>Act One, You Come And Go, The Waiters Remain in which we hear a few words from the D.H. Lawrence of not doing it.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，《你来去，侍者留》。在这一幕中，我们将听到几句来自“不做之事的D.H.劳伦斯”的感言。

00:02:11.830 --> 00:02:25.240
<v Ira Glass>Act Two, Dance. A scientific investigation by Mr. Danny Hoch into what exactly is happening between two people, step by step, moment by moment, when they're not falling in love.
<v Ira Glass>第二幕，《舞蹈》。丹尼·霍克（Danny Hoch）先生的一项科学调查，深入研究了两个人之间究竟发生了什么——一步一步，一刻一刻——当他们没有坠入爱河的时候。

00:02:25.240 --> 00:02:42.150
<v Ira Glass>Act Three, Same Time Next Year. A couple nearly gets together, but does not. And then an uncanny coincidence intervenes, as in a fable, as in a dream, as in a movie. But what does it mean?
<v Ira Glass>第三幕，《明年此时》。一对情侣差点在一起，但没有。接着，一个不可思议的巧合介入了，就像在寓言中，在梦中，在电影中一样。但这到底意味着什么？

00:02:42.150 --> 00:03:02.870
<v Ira Glass>Act Four, Saying No For 75 Years. The story of what happens when you say no to exactly the wrong person at the wrong time when you're 21 years old and looking at prison. Stay with us. Don't say no now.
<v Ira Glass>第四幕，《拒绝了75年》。当你在21岁、面临牢狱之灾时，拒绝了一个绝对错误的人，会发生什么故事。请留下来。现在别拒绝。

00:03:02.870 --> 00:03:16.950
<v Ira Glass>Act One, You Come And Go, The Waiters Remain. We begin today's program with this story. It's a part story, part philosophical inquiry into the nature of not doing things from Geoff Dyer.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，《你来去，侍者留》。我们从这个故事开始今天的节目。这是一篇兼具故事和哲学探讨的文章，关于杰夫·戴尔（Geoff Dyer）笔下“不做之事”的本质。

00:03:16.950 --> 00:03:45.810
<v Geoff Dyer>For years, I wanted to write a book about D.H. Lawrence, a homage to the writer who'd made me want to become a writer. It was a cherished ambition. And as part of my preparation for realizing this cherished ambition, I'd avoided reading anything by Lawrence so that at some point in the future, I could go back to him, if not afresh, then at least not rock-stale. Then after years of avoiding Lawrence, I moved into the phase of what might be termed "pre-preparation."
<v Geoff Dyer>多年来，我想写一本关于D.H.劳伦斯的书，向这位让我渴望成为作家的作家致敬。这是一个珍贵的抱负。为了实现这个珍贵的抱负，我做了些准备，我刻意避免阅读劳伦斯的任何作品，这样在未来的某个时刻，我就能以全新的——或者至少是不那么陈旧疲惫的眼光去读他。在避开劳伦斯多年后，我进入了可能被称为“预备阶段”的时期。

00:03:45.810 --> 00:04:04.130
<v Geoff Dyer>I visited Eastwood, his birthplace. I read biographies. I amassed a horde of photographs, which I kept in a once-new document wallet, blue, on which I had written "D.H.L. Photos" in determined, black ink. I even built up an impressive stack of notes with Lawrence vaguely in mind.
<v Geoff Dyer>我参观了他的出生地伊斯特伍德，阅读了传记。我收集了一大堆照片，把它们放在一个曾经崭新的蓝色文件夹里，上面用坚定的黑色墨水写着“D.H.L.照片”。我甚至凭着对劳伦斯的模糊印象，积累了一叠令人印象深刻的笔记。

00:04:04.130 --> 00:04:43.830
<v Geoff Dyer>But these notes, it's obvious to me now, actually served not to prepare for and facilitate the writing of a book about Lawrence, but to defer and postpone doing so. There's nothing unusual about this. All over the world, people are taking notes as a way of postponing, putting off, and standing in for. My case was more extreme. For not only was taking notes about Lawrence a way of putting off writing a study of and homage to the writer who'd made me want to become a writer, but this study I was putting off writing was itself a way of putting off and postponing another book.
<v Geoff Dyer>但现在很明显，这些笔记实际上并没有为写书做准备或提供便利，而是为了拖延和推迟这件事。这并不罕见。全世界的人都在通过做笔记的方式来拖延、推迟和替代本该做的事。我的情况更为极端。因为记录关于劳伦斯的笔记，不仅是推迟撰写这位让我渴望成为作家的研究和致敬之作的方式，而且这本我一直在推迟写的书，本身也是为了推迟和拖延另一本书。

00:04:43.830 --> 00:05:27.670
<v Geoff Dyer>Although I'd made up my mind to write a book about Lawrence, I'd also made up my mind to write a novel. Writing them both at the same time was inconceivable. And so these two equally overwhelming ambitions first wore each other down and then wiped each other out. As soon as I thought about working on the novel, I fell to thinking that it would be much more enjoyable to write my study of Lawrence. As soon as I started making notes on Lawrence, I realized I was probably sabotaging forever any chance of writing my novel, which, more than any other book I'd written, had to be written immediately before another protracted bout of labor came between me and the idea for what I perceived as a rambling, sub-Bernhardtian rant of a novel.
<v Geoff Dyer>虽然我下定决心要写一本关于劳伦斯的书，但也下定决心要写一部小说。同时写这两本书是不可想象的。因此，这两个同样令人难以抗拒的雄心壮志，先是互相消耗，然后互相消灭。每当我想到要写小说时，我就觉得写关于劳伦斯的研究会更有趣。每当我开始做劳伦斯的笔记时，我就意识到我可能永远破坏了写小说的机会，而这部小说比我写过的任何其他书都更需要立即动笔，以免另一段漫长的劳作介入我与那个想法之间——那个我心目中凌乱、近乎伯恩哈德式的长篇大论的小说。

00:05:27.670 --> 00:06:01.410
<v Geoff Dyer>It was now or never. So I went from making notes on Lawrence to making notes for my novel, by which I mean I went from not working on my book about Lawrence to not working on the novel. Because all of this to-ing, and fro-ing, and note taking actually meant that I never did any work on either book. All I did was switch between two empty files on my computer, one conveniently called C:\DHL, the other C:\NOVEL, and sent myself pinging back and forth between them until, after an hour and a half of this, I would turn off the computer.
<v Geoff Dyer>机不可失，失不再来。所以我从做劳伦斯的笔记转而去做小说的笔记，意思是说我从“不写关于劳伦斯的书”转变成了“不写小说”。因为所有这些来来回回的折腾和做笔记，实际上意味着我从来没有真正动笔写过任何一本书。我所做的只是在电脑上的两个空白文档之间切换，一个方便地命名为C:\DHL，另一个命名为C:\NOVEL，让自己在它们之间来回弹跳，直到一个半小时后，我就把电脑关了。

00:06:01.410 --> 00:06:24.300
<v Geoff Dyer>Because the worst thing of all, I knew, was to wear myself out in this way. The best thing was to do nothing, to sit calmly. But there was no calm of course. Instead, I felt totally desolate because I realized that I was going to write neither my study of D.H. Lawrence nor my novel.
<v Geoff Dyer>因为我知道，最糟糕的事情就是这样耗尽自己。最好的办法是无所事事，冷静地坐着。但当然，根本没有平静可言。相反，我感到极其沮丧，因为我意识到我既不会写关于D.H.劳伦斯的研究，也不会写我的小说。

00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:51.980
<v Geoff Dyer>One of the reasons that it was impossible to get started on either the Lawrence book or the novel was because I was so preoccupied with where to live. I could live anywhere. There were no constraints on me. And because of this, it was impossible to choose. It's easy to make choices when you have things hampering you. A job, kid's schools. But when all you have to go on is your own desires, then life becomes considerably more difficult, not to say intolerable.
<v Geoff Dyer>无法开始劳伦斯那本书或小说的一个原因是，我太专注于住在哪里了。我可以住在任何地方。我没有任何束缚。正因为如此，选择变得不可能。当你受困于工作、孩子学校等琐事时，做选择很容易。但当你只能依靠自己的欲望时，生活就变得相当困难，甚至无法忍受。

00:06:51.980 --> 00:07:15.860
<v Geoff Dyer>Even money wasn't an issue since, at this stage, I was living in Paris. And nowhere could have been more expensive than Paris. The exchange rate got worse by the month, and Paris became more expensive by the month. What the money situation in Paris did was to emphasize that although I had settled in Paris, really, I'd just been passing through extremely slowly.
<v Geoff Dyer>甚至钱也不是问题，因为在这个阶段，我住在巴黎。没有什么地方比巴黎更昂贵了。汇率逐月恶化，巴黎的生活费逐月上涨。巴黎的财务状况强调了一点：虽然我在巴黎安顿下来，但实际上，我只是在极其缓慢地经过这里。

00:07:15.860 --> 00:07:32.170
<v Geoff Dyer>That is all anyone English or American can do in Paris, pass through. You may spend 10 years passing through, but essentially you're still a sightseer, a tourist. You come and go. The waiters remain.
<v Geoff Dyer>这就是所有英国人或美国人在巴黎能做的事情：经过。你可能会花10年时间在这里经过，但本质上你仍然是一个观光客、游客。你来来去去。侍者们依然在那里。

00:07:32.170 --> 00:08:07.760
<v Geoff Dyer>The longer I stayed, the more powerful it became, this feeling that I was just passing through. I'd thought about subscribing to the cable channel, Canal Plus, as a way of making myself feel more settled. But what was the point in subscribing to Canal Plus when, in all probability, I would be moving on in a few months? Obviously, the way to make myself more settled was to acquire some of the trappings of permanence. But there never seemed any point acquiring the aptly-named trappings of permanence when, in a couple of months, I might be moving on, might well be moving on, would almost certainly be moving on?
<v Geoff Dyer>我待的时间越长，这种“我只是经过”的感觉就越强烈。我曾考虑订阅有线频道Canal Plus，以此让自己感觉更安定些。但考虑到很可能几个月后我就会搬走，订阅Canal Plus又有什么意义呢？显然，让自己安顿下来的方法是获取一些“永恒的附属品”。但考虑到几个月后我可能就会搬走，获取那些恰如其名“永恒的附属品”似乎总是毫无意义，可能真的会搬走，而且几乎可以肯定会搬走。

00:08:07.760 --> 00:08:44.920
<v Geoff Dyer>Because there was nothing to keep me where I was. Had I acquired some of the trappings of permanence, I might have stayed put. But I never acquired any of the trappings of permanence because I knew that the moment these trappings had been acquired, I would seized with the desire to leave, to move on. And I would then have to free myself from these trappings. And so, lacking any of the trappings of permanence, I was perpetually on the brink of potential departure. That was the only way I could remain anywhere, to be constantly on the brink, not of actual, but of potential departure.
<v Geoff Dyer>因为没有什么能留住我。如果我获取了一些永恒的附属品，我或许就能安顿下来。但我从来没有获取过任何永恒的附属品，因为我知道一旦拥有了这些东西，我就会被离开、搬走的欲望所攫住。然后我就不得不摆脱这些羁绊。所以，因为缺乏任何永恒的附属品，我永远处于即将离开的边缘。这是我能留在任何地方的唯一方式，那就是时刻保持在一种——不是实际上的，而是潜在的——离开的边缘。

00:08:44.920 --> 00:09:29.570
<v Geoff Dyer>These were all issues I intended to address in different ways, either in mediated form in my study of Lawrence, or directly in my novel, or vice versa. But there was an additional practical complication too. Since I was obliged to spend a certain amount of time away from wherever I lived, and since the rent on my Paris apartment was so high, and because of the exchange rate was becoming higher every month, I was frequently obliged to sublet it. Strictly speaking, to sub-sublet it since I was subletting it myself. And since if you are subletting your apartment, you do not want to acquire too many valuable or personal items, which might get destroyed, it then comes about that you, yourself, are living in conditions arranged primarily for those subletting from you.
<v Geoff Dyer>这些都是我打算用不同方式解决的问题，要么以中介形式写在我的劳伦斯研究中，要么直接写在我的小说里，反之亦然。但还有一个实际的复杂因素。由于我必须在远离住所的地方待上一段时间，再加上巴黎公寓的租金很高，而且汇率每月都在上涨，我经常不得不转租它。严格来说，是转租给别人，因为我自己就是租客。而且由于转租公寓时，你不希望放置太多贵重或个人的物品以免损坏，结果就是你自己住在主要为转租客安排的环境中。

00:09:29.570 --> 00:10:05.120
<v Geoff Dyer>Effectively, you are subletting from yourself. That's what I was doing, subletting from myself. Strictly speaking, sub-subletting, living in an apartment devoid of anything that might have made it my apartment in the sense of "my home." I'd conspired to arrange for myself the worst of all possible worlds. And my days were spent in this unbreakable circle of anxiety, always going over the same ground again and again, always with some new variable, but never with any change.
<v Geoff Dyer>实际上，你在从自己手里转租。我当时就在做这件事，从自己手中转租。严格来说，是二次转租，住在一间空荡荡的公寓里，没有任何东西能让它成为“我的家”。我合谋为自己安排了最糟糕的情况。我的日子就在这种无法打破的焦虑圈中度过，总是不断地重走旧路，总是带着一点新的变量，却从来没有任何改变。

00:10:05.120 --> 00:10:57.040
<v Geoff Dyer>I had to do something to break this circle. And so I decided to sign a contract that would make me the official tenant, as opposed to the illegal subtenant. I wasn't even sure that I wanted to stay in an apartment where I'd actually been extremely unhappy for 90% of my stay, where 90% of my stay had been dominated by anxiety about, A, whether I was going to stay, and B, whether I was going to start a novel or start my study of Lawrence. But as soon as the managing agent said that they were unwilling to let the place to me, a foreigner with no job and no steady income, I became convinced that I had to stay in this apartment where I'd actually been sublimely happy. That there was, in fact, nowhere else on earth where I could hope to be as content. Eventually, my rich friend, Herve Landry, "Money Landry," as I like to call him, agreed to stand as guarantor.
<v Geoff Dyer>我必须做点什么来打破这个循环。所以我决定签署一份合同，让我成为正式租户，而不是非法的二房东。我甚至不确定自己是否想住在一间我已经住了90%时间都极其不快乐的公寓里，我大部分时间都在焦虑：A，我是否会留下；B，我是否会开始写小说或开始我的劳伦斯研究。但当代理商说他们不愿把房子租给我这个没有工作、没有稳定收入的外国人时，我变得确信我必须留在这个我其实一直非常快乐的公寓里。事实上，在这个世界上，我不可能希望在别处过得更好了。最终，我那有钱的朋友埃尔韦·兰德里（Herve Landry），我喜欢叫他“钱财兰德里”，同意做我的担保人。

00:10:57.040 --> 00:11:41.170
<v Geoff Dyer>The managing agents relented, and I signed the lease that made me the official locataire. I was ecstatic for about five minutes. Then I realized I'd taken on an awesome, not to say, crippling responsibility. And far from solving the problem of where to live, I'd actually put a lid on it, so that now my uncertainty was boiling away under pressure, threatening to blow me apart. The one thing I could be sure of was that I had to leave this apartment where I'd never known a moment's peace of mind as soon as possible. If I stayed here, I saw now, I would fail to write both my novel and my study of Lawrence. That much was obvious.
<v Geoff Dyer>代理商妥协了，我签署了租约，成为正式承租人。我兴奋了大约五分钟。然后我意识到我承担了一项可怕的、甚至是令人崩溃的责任。我不仅没有解决住在哪里这个问题，反而给它加了个盖子，现在我的不确定性在压力下沸腾，威胁着要让我崩溃。我可以确定的一件事是，我必须尽快离开这个让我从未有过片刻安宁的公寓。我现在明白了，如果我留在这里，我既写不成小说，也写不成关于劳伦斯的研究。这一点很明显。

00:11:41.170 --> 00:12:14.070
<v Geoff Dyer>Round and round I went, making no progress, resolving one thing one moment and another the next. I wrote to the agents and officially renounced the flat, claiming that "professional reasons" had obliged me to return to England. The agents wrote back, acknowledging my decision to leave the apartment. I wrote back, saying that, "Professional reasons now oblige me to remain in Paris. Could I, therefore, unrenounce my apartment?" Relieved to be free of the trouble of re-letting it, the agents agreed to let me remain in the apartment, which I had just renounced.
<v Geoff Dyer>我转来转去，毫无进展，一会儿决定这样，一会儿决定那样。我写信给代理商正式退租，声称“专业原因”迫使我回到英国。代理商回信，确认了我离开公寓的决定。我回信说：“专业原因现在迫使我留在巴黎。因此，我能撤回退租申请吗？”代理商为了省去重新招租的麻烦，同意让我继续留在刚刚被我退掉的公寓里。

00:12:14.070 --> 00:12:37.570
<v Geoff Dyer>And so it went on. I wrote again to renounce the apartment definitively. They sent a somewhat curt acknowledgement of my decision. I wrote back, changing my definitive decision to leave to a definitive decision to stay. But it was too late. I had to leave.
<v Geoff Dyer>事情就这样继续下去。我又写信明确退租。他们发来了一封语气有些生硬的确认函。我回信说，我把离开的最终决定改为了留下的最终决定。但已经太晚了。我必须离开。

00:12:37.570 --> 00:12:58.790
<v Geoff Dyer>Now that I did have to leave, I was faced with the terrible prospect of having nowhere to live, of having to decide where to live without delay. And only then did I realize how much this apartment meant to me, how it had actually become my home. Although I believed that I'd hardly any of my things in this apartment, there were actually many of my own things that I now had to find a place for.
<v Geoff Dyer>现在我真的必须离开了，我面临着一个可怕的前景：无处可去，必须立即决定住在哪里。也就在那时，我才意识到这个公寓对我意味着什么，它实际上已经成了我的家。虽然我认为公寓里几乎没有什么我的东西，但实际上我有很多个人物品，现在必须找到地方安放。

00:12:58.790 --> 00:13:12.270
<v Geoff Dyer>Over the years, I'd actually acquired quite a few of the trappings of permanence. I even owned a surprising amount of furniture, some of it rather nice. Where was I going to store it? And what about me? Where was I going to store myself?
<v Geoff Dyer>多年来，我实际上获取了不少永恒的附属品。我甚至拥有数量惊人的家具，其中一些还相当不错。我该把它们存放在哪儿？还有我呢？我该把自己存放在哪儿？

00:13:12.270 --> 00:13:46.160
<v Geoff Dyer>Rome was a possibility. Laura, my almost-wife, had a lovely apartment in Rome and was always arguing in favor of our settling there. I fretted and wondered. Why was I even prevaricating like this? I was mad not to go to Rome. Rome was in Italy, the country where the Lawrences had spent more time than any other. If I was to stand any chance of making any progress with my study of Lawrence, it was probably the very best place I could be.
<v Geoff Dyer>罗马是一个选择。劳拉，我那几乎算作妻子的爱人，在罗马有一间可爱的公寓，总是主张我们在那里定居。我焦虑着，纠结着。为什么我甚至要这样推托呢？我不去罗马简直是疯了。罗马在意大利，这是劳伦斯夫妇花费时间最多的国家。如果我想要在劳伦斯研究上取得任何进展，那很可能是我所能待的最好的地方。

00:13:46.160 --> 00:14:01.220
<v Geoff Dyer>As soon as I arrived, I knew I'd made the right decision. My mind was made up. I was ready to begin my study of D.H. Lawrence. The only trouble was the heat. The heat was tremendous. And nowhere in Rome was hotter than Laura's apartment.
<v Geoff Dyer>我一到那里，就知道我做了正确的决定。我已经下定决心。我准备开始我的D.H.劳伦斯研究了。唯一的麻烦是天气。天气热得要命。而在罗马，没有任何地方比劳拉的公寓更热。

00:14:01.220 --> 00:14:32.240
<v Geoff Dyer>Even the light was hot. We tried to keep the light at bay, but it drilled through the keyhole, squeezed under the door, levered open the smallest of cracks in the shutters. My mind was made up. I was ready to work. But it was too hot to work. It was so hot, we spent our waking hours dozing and our sleeping hours lying awake, trying to sleep. We were in a kind of trance.
<v Geoff Dyer>连光线都带着热度。我们试图阻挡光线，但它从锁孔钻入，从门缝挤进，撬开百叶窗最细小的缝隙。我下定决心。我准备好工作了。但实在太热，没法工作。天气太热了，我们醒着的时候都在打盹，睡着的时候却躺着清醒，试图入睡。我们处于一种恍惚状态。

00:14:32.240 --> 00:15:07.800
<v Geoff Dyer>The perfect life, the perfect lie, is one which prevents you from doing that which you would ideally have done, painted, say, or written unpublishable poetry, but which, in fact, you've no wish to do. People need to feel that they've been thwarted by circumstances from pursuing the life which, had they led it, they would not have wanted. Whereas the life they really want is precisely a compound of all those thwarting circumstances. It's a very elaborate, extremely simple procedure, arranging this web of self-deceit, contriving to convince yourself that you were prevented from doing what you wanted.
<v Geoff Dyer>完美的生活，完美的谎言，就是那种阻止你做你本可以做到的事情（比如画画，或者写些无法出版的诗歌），但事实上你根本没有愿望去做的事情。人们需要感到自己因为环境阻碍而无法追求某种生活，如果真的过上了那种生活，他们反而不会想要。而他们真正想要的生活，恰恰是所有那些阻碍因素的集合。这是一个非常复杂却又极其简单的程序，编织起这种自欺欺人的网，让自己确信是外界阻止了你做你想做的事。

00:15:07.800 --> 00:15:18.220
<v Geoff Dyer>Most people don't want what they want. People want to be prevented, restricted. The hamster not only loves his cage, he'd be lost without it.
<v Geoff Dyer>大多数人并不想要他们声称想要的东西。人们想要的是被阻止、被限制。仓鼠不仅爱它的笼子，没有笼子它反而会迷失。

00:15:18.220 --> 00:15:46.720
<v Geoff Dyer>That's why children are so convenient. You have children because you're struggling to get by as an artist or failing to get on with your career. Then you can persuade yourself that your children prevented you from having this career that never looked like working out. And so it goes on. Things are always forsaken in the name of an obligation to someone else, never as a failing, a falling short of yourself.
<v Geoff Dyer>这就是为什么孩子这么方便。你生孩子是因为你作为艺术家在挣扎，或者事业不顺。然后你可以说服自己是孩子阻止了你实现那个本来就没什么希望的事业。事情总是以对他人的义务为名而被抛弃，而不是因为自己的失败或不足。

00:15:46.720 --> 00:16:10.270
<v Geoff Dyer>I've devoted more of my life to thoughts of giving up than anyone else I can think of. Nietzsche wrote that the thought of suicide had got him through many a bad night. And thinking of giving up is probably the one thing that's kept me going. I think about it on a daily basis, but always come up against the problem of what to do when I've given up. Give up one thing, and you're immediately obliged to do something else.
<v Geoff Dyer>我一生中在“放弃”这个念头上花费的时间，比我能想到的任何人都多。尼采写道，自杀的念头让他度过了许多糟糕的夜晚。而考虑放弃可能是我坚持下去的唯一动力。我每天都会想这件事，但总是面临“放弃之后做什么”的问题。放弃了一件事，你立即就得做另一件事。

00:16:10.270 --> 00:16:56.150
<v Geoff Dyer>Let's suppose, for example, that I decided to call it a day, to give up, to abandon any attempt not just at earning a living, but having a life. But what then? What would happen next? Within five minutes, I'd be thinking about listening to music and would put a CD on the stereo. Five minutes after that, I'd be up again because I would have grown fed up with that piece of music and would be scanning the shelves and shelves of CDs, searching in vain for a piece of music that I was not heartily sick of, thinking to myself that if I had more CDs, there would surely be one that I would like to listen to. And before I knew it, I'd be out of the house and on my way to the megastore, looking for a new CD.
<v Geoff Dyer>举个例子，假设我决定收手、放弃、停止一切努力，不仅是不再为了谋生，甚至不再为了生活。那然后呢？接下来会发生什么？五分钟内，我就会想听音乐，把CD放进音响。再过五分钟，我就又会站起来，因为我对那段音乐厌倦了，开始扫描架子上的一排排CD，徒劳地寻找一张我不感到极其厌恶的音乐，心想如果我有多一点CD，肯定会有我喜欢听的。没过多久，我就已经出门去超级商店买新CD了。

00:16:56.150 --> 00:17:31.030
<v Geoff Dyer>Should anyone flatter us by asking what we're looking for, what we are searching for, then we think immediately, almost instinctively in vast terms. God, fulfillment, love. But our lives are actually made up of lots of tiny searches for things like a CD we are not sick of, an out-of-print edition of Phoenix, a picture of Lawrence that I saw when I was 17, another identical pair of suede shoes to the ones that I'm wearing now. Add them together, and these little things make up an epic quest, more than enough for one lifetime.
<v Geoff Dyer>如果有人奉承我们，问我们在寻找什么，我们在追求什么，我们会立刻、近乎本能地从宏大的角度去思考。上帝，成就感，爱。但我们的生活实际上是由许多小小的搜寻组成的，比如一张我们不厌倦的CD，一本绝版的《凤凰》，一张我17岁时见过的劳伦斯照片，或者一双跟我现在穿的一模一样的麂皮鞋。把这些加在一起，这些小事构成了一场史诗般的探索，足以填满一生。

00:17:31.030 --> 00:18:23.820
<v Geoff Dyer>Thinking specifically of the search for CDs, let's assume that after deciding to give up, after sitting around listening to CDs and going out to buy a new CD, I found a CD I liked the idea of listening to. Still, at some point, I would not simply grow tired of listening to this new CD, but would actually become heartily sick of the idea of listening to CDs and would think to myself that sitting around listening to CDs is a much more enjoyable activity, a much more enjoyable inactivity if it is a relief from something else, anything else. And so I would resign myself to picking up my pen and trying once again if for no other reason than to render listening to my CDs a little less dispiriting, to make some progress with my study of D.H. Lawrence.
<v Geoff Dyer>特别想到寻找CD这件事，假设在我决定放弃之后，在坐着听CD和出门买新CD之后，我找到了一张我想听的CD。即便如此，在某个时刻，我不仅会对听这张新CD感到厌倦，而且会真正厌倦“听CD”这个想法，心想坐着听CD是一种更令人愉快的活动——如果它是一种解脱的话，从其他任何事情中解脱出来。所以我不得不拿起笔再次尝试，哪怕只是为了让听音乐变得不那么令人沮丧，以便在我的D.H.劳伦斯研究上取得一点进展。

00:18:23.820 --> 00:19:06.040
<v Geoff Dyer>And there you have it. One way or another, we all have to write our studies of D.H. Lawrence even if they will never be published, even if we will never complete them, even if all we are left with after years and years of effort is an unfinished, unfinishable record of how we failed to live up to our own earlier ambitions. Still, we all have to try to make some progress with our books about D.H. Lawrence. The world over, from Taos to Taormina, from the places we have visited to countries we will never set foot in, the best we can do is to try to make some progress with our studies of D.H. Lawrence.
<v Geoff Dyer>就是这样。不管怎样，我们每个人都必须写我们的D.H.劳伦斯研究，即使它们永远不会出版，即使我们永远完成不了，即使经过多年的努力，我们留下的只是一份未完成的、无法完成的记录——记录我们如何辜负了自己早期的雄心。即便如此，我们每个人都必须努力在关于D.H.劳伦斯的书上取得一些进展。全世界，从陶斯到陶尔米纳，从我们去过的地方到我们永远不会涉足的国家，我们能做的最好的事，就是努力在我们的D.H.劳伦斯研究上取得一点进展。

00:19:06.040 --> 00:19:15.050
<v Ira Glass>Geoff Dyer reading an excerpt from his amazing, little book Out of Sheer Rage. He's also the author of Paris Trance and But Beautiful, a book about jazz.
<v Ira Glass>刚才杰夫·戴尔朗读了他那本精彩的小书《纯粹愤怒之外》（Out of Sheer Rage）的节选。他还是《巴黎恍惚》（Paris Trance）和关于爵士乐的书《依然美丽》（But Beautiful）的作者。

