<v Ira Glass>Taking the place of, using the energy that could be devoted to--
<v Ira Glass>代替了，利用本来可以投入到——的能量。

00:31:15.210 --> 00:31:47.390
<v Amanda Marks>Well, but I was very serious. I was very excited that he liked me for my mind, that we had this connection, that it wasn't about that. But finally, the sexual side got a little sticky. And my last encounter with him was just uncomfortable. The fact was he was 21. I was 14. I wasn't going to go to his house alone. And that was pretty much a deal breaker.
<v Amanda Marks>嗯，我当时很认真。我很兴奋他喜欢我的头脑，我们有这种联系，而不是因为那些。但最终，身体层面变得有点尴尬。我和他的最后一次接触真的很不舒服。事实是他21岁。我14岁。我不会单独去他家。那几乎是一个底线。

00:31:47.390 --> 00:31:58.600
<v Amanda Marks>OK. It's about 12 years later. I'm back in New York City for some family stuff. I'm wandering through Central Park. I don't really have anything to do that night. And suddenly, I get this flash.
<v Amanda Marks>好。大约12年后。我为了家里的事回到纽约。我在中央公园闲逛。那天晚上我没什么事可做。突然，我产生了一种预感。

00:31:58.600 --> 00:32:05.660
<v Amanda Marks>I'm like, it's summer. It's about 5 o'clock. Shakespeare in the Park. So I wander over to the Delacorte.
<v Amanda Marks>我想，是夏天。大约5点钟。公园莎士比亚戏剧节。所以我漫步到了德拉科特（Delacorte）。

00:32:05.660 --> 00:32:18.580
<v Amanda Marks>And there's just this endless, endless line. It's just huge. And I get all the way on the end of it. And it's clear to me that I'm not going to get in. And so I come up with this scheme to cut to the front of the line. So I go from the end to fourth.
<v Amanda Marks>那里排着无穷无尽的长队。太壮观了。我走到队尾。很明显我进不去了。所以我发明了一个计划，插队到前面。我从队尾跑到了第四个位置。

00:32:18.580 --> 00:32:38.890
<v Amanda Marks>And I see this really, really, really cute guy pull the exact same line-cutting ploy that I did. The exact same thing. And he's really cute. And so I catch his eye. And I walk over, and I say, "You know, I did the exact same thing." And he goes, "Yeah, I don't know why anybody waits in line. I always do this. It's great."
<v Amanda Marks>然后我看到一个非常、非常、非常可爱的男孩用了和我一模一样的插队伎俩。完全一样。他很可爱。于是我跟他对视了一下，走过去说：“你知道吗，我也做了同样的事。”他说：“是啊，不知道为什么有人要排队。我总是这样。太棒了。”

00:32:38.890 --> 00:32:52.120
<v Amanda Marks>So we start chatting. He's got a blanket. I've got some fruit.
<v Amanda Marks>我们开始聊天。他带着毯子。我带着些水果。

00:32:52.120 --> 00:33:04.290
<v Amanda Marks>And we're chatting and eating away, perfectly comfortable conversation. He asked me my name. I tell him. He tells me his name. And then he tells me his last name. And I recognize it.
<v Amanda Marks>我们聊着天，吃着东西，对话非常舒服。他问我的名字。我告诉了他。他告诉了我他的名字。然后他说了他的姓。我认出来了。

00:33:04.290 --> 00:33:24.700
<v Amanda Marks>I can't not recognize it. Because his name was always really uncomfortable for me. His name is a synonym for the male anatomy. But he pronounces it differently. And it just always made me really uncomfortable when I was 14.
<v Amanda Marks>我无法不认出来。因为他的姓总是让我感到很不舒服。他的姓是男性器官的同义词。但他发音不同。这让我14岁的时候总是感到非常尴尬。

00:33:24.700 --> 00:33:37.250
<v Amanda Marks>I pause, and my heart starts pounding. My mouth dries up. And I say, "Over 12 years ago, you and I picked each other up in line for Rolling Stones tickets." And he stares at me.
<v Amanda Marks>我停顿了一下，心跳加速。嘴里发干。我说：“12年前，我们曾在滚石乐队排队买票时认识。”他盯着我。

00:33:37.250 --> 00:33:44.910
<v Amanda Marks>And he totally knows who I am. His jaw drops. He completely remembers. He can't believe it. I can't believe it.
<v Amanda Marks>他完全知道我是谁。他下巴都掉了。他完全记得。他不敢相信。我也不敢相信。

00:33:44.910 --> 00:33:55.550
<v Amanda Marks>And it's just so romantic. What could be more romantic? We're in Central Park. We're on a blanket. We're about to go see Shakespeare in the Park. It's like it's destiny.
<v Amanda Marks>这真是太浪漫了。还有什么比这更浪漫的呢？我们在中央公园。我们在毯子上。我们要去看公园莎士比亚戏剧了。这简直是宿命。

00:33:55.550 --> 00:34:00.160
<v Amanda Marks>Oh my god, and the play? All's Well That Ends Well.
<v Amanda Marks>哦天哪，剧名呢？《皆大欢喜》（All's Well That Ends Well）。

00:34:00.160 --> 00:34:05.290
<v Ira Glass>So it's all symbolic. It's all coming together. It's all magical. And all forming around one thought.
<v Ira Glass>所以这全是象征性的。一切都汇聚在一起。一切都很神奇。而且都是围绕一个念头形成的。

00:34:05.290 --> 00:34:26.500
<v Amanda Marks>Oh, it just could not be more perfect. I'm thinking, this is the story you tell the grandchildren. I love romantic comedies. I write romantic comedies. It's just like this is what happens in the movies. I'm so excited.
