<v Amanda Marks>是啊。他没做出什么成就。

00:35:12.790 --> 00:35:28.700
<v Ira Glass>That's interesting because when there's that kind of coincidence of running into somebody again, we think automatically that it should turn out to be romantic. But it could be that you just meet some people again, just have exactly the same experience over again.
<v Ira Glass>这很有意思，因为当这种巧合发生再次遇到某人时，我们自动认为那应该是浪漫的。但可能你只是再次遇到了某些人，拥有完全一样的体验。

00:35:28.700 --> 00:35:49.520
<v Amanda Marks>Yeah, yeah. In case you didn't learn that lesson once. So it just got yuckier. I left eventually. And he would call me randomly, drunk, at 11:30 at night, from some bar, like, "What are you doing?"
<v Amanda Marks>是啊，是啊。以防你上次没学会教训。事情变得越来越恶心。我最终离开了。他会随机地在半夜11:30喝醉了给我打电话，在酒吧里说：“你在干嘛？”

00:35:49.520 --> 00:35:59.510
<v Ira Glass>And so this whole thing stayed in the "We didn't" stage. You just never went forward out of "We didn't." It was sort of like you were reunited to re-experience the "We didn't."
<v Ira Glass>所以这一切都停留在“我们没做”的阶段。你从未从“我们没做”阶段向前迈进。就像你们重聚只是为了重温“我们没做”的体验。

00:35:59.510 --> 00:36:17.150
<v Amanda Marks>Yeah. At first, it seemed like there was this big cosmic thing keeping us apart, age. There's so few things to actually keep couples apart anymore. You know what I mean? I write romantic comedies, and it's really hard to come up with a reason that people don't just jump into bed in the first 20 minutes.
<v Amanda Marks>是啊。起初，似乎有一种宏大的宇宙力量让我们分开，年龄。现在几乎没有什么能真正阻碍情侣了。你知道我的意思吗？我写浪漫喜剧，真的很难找到一个理由，让人们不在前20分钟就滚床单。

00:36:17.150 --> 00:36:18.670
<v Ira Glass>Oh, come. Are you serious?
<v Ira Glass>噢，真的吗？你是认真的？

00:36:18.670 --> 00:36:30.510
<v Amanda Marks>Yeah, it's hard. Sure. When you're doing the whole "It's kismet, and we know that they're meant to be," yeah, sure, that's hard to sustain dramatically. That's why most romantic comedies are so boring and predictable.
<v Amanda Marks>是啊，很难。当然。当你表现出“这就是宿命，注定在一起”的时候，那很难在戏剧性上维持。这就是为什么大多数浪漫喜剧如此无聊和可预测。

00:36:30.510 --> 00:36:32.000
<v Ira Glass>Because there's not enough keeping them apart.
<v Ira Glass>因为没有足够的阻碍让它们分开。

00:36:32.000 --> 00:36:54.690
<v Amanda Marks>It's like, oh, he's engaged to some other girl who's really stuck up and comes from this rich family. And we know that he's never going to end up with this stuck-up girl. Do you know what I mean? But they go through the motions of he has to figure it out that she's not right for him. And you know I mean? That's why most romantic comedies are so middling and why it's really hard to make a good one.
<v Amanda Marks>比如，哦，他跟另一个爱摆架子的富家女订婚了。我们知道他永远不会和那个爱摆架子的女孩在一起。你知道我的意思吗？但他们必须走过场，他必须意识到她不适合自己。你知道我的意思吗？这就是为什么大多数浪漫喜剧都平庸至极，为什么很难拍出一部好的。

00:36:54.690 --> 00:37:08.570
<v Amanda Marks>So we had that. We had age. It was pretty dramatic. It was very real then.
<v Amanda Marks>所以我们有那个。我们有年龄问题。那当时非常有戏剧性。那时候很真实。

00:37:08.570 --> 00:37:12.240
<v Ira Glass>I'm surprised at what you're saying about romantic comedies.
<v Ira Glass>我对你关于浪漫喜剧的评论感到惊讶。

00:37:12.240 --> 00:37:34.400
<v Amanda Marks>But I guess what I mean when I say it's hard now, compared to the heyday of Hollywood-- when you think of the classic romantic comedies, those were written under the Hays code. And you could not have two people going to bed together unless they were married. And so it was an obstacle in that very direct, obvious way. And you needed parent approval, and you needed to be of the same class, and all of that.
<v Amanda Marks>但我猜我说“现在很难”的意思是，相比好莱坞的黄金时代——当你想到经典的浪漫喜剧时，那是根据《海斯法典》（Hays code）编写的。在结婚之前，你不能让两个人上床。所以那是一个非常直接、明显的障碍。你需要家长的许可，你需要门当户对等等。

00:37:34.400 --> 00:37:42.270
<v Amanda Marks>And now, with sexual mores so changed, that's really the thing. It's like, well, what's stopping these two people from going to bed?
<v Amanda Marks>而现在，性道德已经改变，问题就在于：究竟是什么在阻止这两个人上床？

00:37:42.270 --> 00:37:55.150
<v Ira Glass>But what you're saying somehow is that we, as a people, spend less time in the realm of "We didn't" than we used to two generations ago.
<v Ira Glass>但你刚才的意思似乎是，与两代人前相比，我们现在在“我们没做”这个境界花费的时间更少了。

00:37:55.150 --> 00:38:00.360
<v Amanda Marks>Yeah, I think now we spend time in "I wish we didn't," "I wish we hadn't."
<v Amanda Marks>是的，我想现在我们花时间在“我希望我们没做”、“我希望我们没发生过”的状态。

00:38:00.360 --> 00:38:03.010
<v Ira Glass>In other words, people actually get involved, but then regret it.
<v Ira Glass>换句话说，人们实际上参与其中，但后来又后悔了。

00:38:03.010 --> 00:38:09.520
<v Amanda Marks>Yeah, sure. That's really the story now.
<v Amanda Marks>是啊，当然。那才是现在的故事。

00:38:09.520 --> 00:38:12.440
<v Ira Glass>Amanda Marks, a screenwriter in Los Angeles.
<v Ira Glass>阿曼达·马克斯，洛杉矶编剧。

00:38:12.440 --> 00:38:34.050
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - "'TIL THE NEXT GOODBYE" BY THE ROLLING STONES]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - 滚石乐队的《直到下一次告别》]

00:38:34.050 --> 00:39:08.240
<v Ira Glass>Act Four, Saying No For 75 Years. For most of us, the stakes of not doing something on any given day are so low that as soon as the moment of decision passes, we never think of it again. It doesn't even linger as a memory. But sometimes, the stakes are so staggeringly high that we spend the rest of our lives in the shadow of that one decision, of one no. No, said at exactly the wrong moment to exactly the wrong person. Steve Bogira is a journalist who stumbled onto a story exactly like that about a guy named Vincent Bogan.
<v Ira Glass>第四幕，《拒绝了75年》。对于大多数人来说，在任何一天不做某事的风险都很小，一旦决定的时刻过去，我们就再也不会想起它了。它甚至不会作为记忆残留。但有时，风险大得惊人，以至于我们要余生都生活在那一个决定、那一个“不”的阴影下。那是一个在极其错误的时刻对极其错误的人说出的“不”。史蒂夫·博吉拉（Steve Bogira）是一位偶然发现了一个类似故事的记者，主人公名叫文森特·博根（Vincent Bogan）。

00:39:08.240 --> 00:39:24.790
<v Steve Bogira>I met Vincent 18 years ago when he was 15, when he was about to become a freshman at Bogan High School. Bogan is just a coincidence. It's the same as his name. Bogan is a high school on Chicago's Southwest side.
<v Steve Bogira>18年前，我在他15岁时认识了他，那时他即将成为博根高中的新生。博根只是个巧合。它和他名字一样。博根是芝加哥南区的一所高中。

00:39:24.790 --> 00:39:48.430
<v Steve Bogira>At the time, it was all white, and Vincent was going to be one of the first black kids to go to this school. And I wanted to write a story for the Chicago Reader about what it'd be like for a black kid to come to that high school. So I rode the bus with him one September day in 1981 and followed him during his first day at Bogan.
<v Steve Bogira>当时，那所学校全是白人，而文森特将是第一批进入这所学校的黑人孩子之一。我想为《芝加哥读者报》（Chicago Reader）写一篇关于一个黑人孩子进入这所高中的故事。所以1981年9月的一天，我坐公交车陪他一起，跟踪了他第一天上学的经过。

00:39:48.430 --> 00:39:54.370
<v Ira Glass>What kind of kid was he at 15?
<v Ira Glass>他在15岁时是个什么样的孩子？

00:39:54.370 --> 00:40:24.750
<v Steve Bogira>The previous spring, he had been voted "Most Dedicated Student" in his eighth grade class, won $50 for that. He wanted to be an architect. And he was just a nice kid. He had a father who worked as a welder. His mother had been killed in a car accident a couple of years before. And he was the kind of kid who cuts the grass and sprinkles the lawn, wasn't the greatest student, but he did graduate.
<v Steve Bogira>前一个春天，他在八年级班里被选为“最敬业学生”，还赢了50美元奖金。他想成为一名建筑师。他是个很好的孩子。他有个父亲是一名焊工。他母亲几年前在一场车祸中丧生。他是个会帮着割草、浇草地的孩子，虽然学习成绩不是最顶尖的，但他确实毕业了。

00:40:24.750 --> 00:40:28.190
<v Ira Glass>So when did you hear of him next? How many years passed?
<v Ira Glass>所以你后来怎么又听说他了？过了多少年？

00:40:28.190 --> 00:40:59.840
<v Steve Bogira>I think it was in 1989. I saw a headline in the Tribune that caught my eye. It said something like, "An armed robber gets 75 years," which is a real long time for someone to do for armed robbery. And I read this story, and it said Vincent Bogan had been charged with 17 armed robberies committed in a six-week period in 1988.
<v Steve Bogira>我想是在1989年。我在《论坛报》（Tribune）上看到的一条标题引起了我的注意。标题写着类似“武装劫匪被判75年”的内容，对于武装抢劫来说，这是一段很长的刑期。我读了这个故事，说文森特·博根被指控在1988年的六周内犯下了17起武装抢劫案。

00:40:59.840 --> 00:41:07.530
<v Steve Bogira>So I thought this is the Vincent who I wrote about. And I decided to find out what had happened.
<v Steve Bogira>所以我认为这就是我写过的那个文森特。我决定找出到底发生了什么。

00:41:07.530 --> 00:41:28.250
<v Ira Glass>What had happened, he found out, is that while Vincent was in high school, his father was laid off from his job as a welder and started dealing cocaine out of the house. He taught Vincent the family business. Not long after that, he died. Vincent became addicted to cocaine, stole to support his habit, and was caught. He was offered a deal by the state's attorney, a typical kind of deal for American courts today.
<v Ira Glass>他发现，文森特在高中时，他父亲被焊工岗位裁员，开始在家里贩卖可卡因。他教文森特家族生意。没过多久，父亲就去世了。文森特染上了可卡因毒瘾，为了支持毒瘾而行窃，最终被捕。地方检察官给了他一个交易，这是今天美国法院里典型的交易方式。

00:41:28.250 --> 00:41:53.330
<v Ira Glass>If he would admit that he was guilty on all 17 counts of armed robbery, which would make a full trial unnecessary, there would be no trial, then he would get a 25-year sentence. This is called-- as anybody who watches cop shows on TV knows-- copping a plea, taking a plea bargain. And this is where Vincent said, no, said, no in a way that changed his life forever, said, no against the advice of everybody in his life. He wanted to go to trial.
<v Ira Glass>如果他承认所有17项武装抢劫罪名成立，这样就不需要审判，没有庭审，他就能得到25年的刑期。这被称为——就像任何看电视警察剧的人都知道的那样——认罪辩诉，接受辩诉交易。这就是文森特说“不”的地方，他以一种永远改变他生命的方式说“不”，违背了他生命中所有人的建议说“不”。他想接受庭审。

00:41:53.330 --> 00:42:03.790
<v Steve Bogira>His lawyer said it was a mistake. He tried to convince Vincent to take the deal, said, "Vincent, you're going to get hammered if you go to trial. You'll get much more than 25 years."
<v Steve Bogira>他的律师说那是个错误。他试图说服文森特接受交易，说：“文森特，如果你去庭审，你会惨败的。你会得到比25年多得多的刑期。”

00:42:03.790 --> 00:42:13.400
<v Ira Glass>He went to trial on 1 of the 17 counts of armed robbery. The judge gave him the maximum sentence, 30 years. He still had 16 counts left, 16 crimes.
<v Ira Glass>他在17项武装抢劫罪名中的一项接受了审判。法官判了他最高刑期，30年。他还有16项指控，16宗犯罪。

00:42:13.400 --> 00:42:32.950
<v Steve Bogira>At some point, it may have been just before sentencing, the state came to Vincent again and said, "Plead guilty to all the rest of these, and we'll give you just a total of 30." Vincent, again, said, "No, I want to go to trial." So he went to trial a second time, got found guilty again by a jury, and got sentenced to another 30.
<v Steve Bogira>在某个时刻，可能就在判刑之前，州政府再次找到文森特说：“承认剩下的所有罪名，我们就总共给你30年。”文森特再次说：“不，我想接受庭审。”所以他第二次接受了审判，再次被陪审团判定有罪，被判了另外30年。

00:42:32.950 --> 00:42:58.620
<v Ira Glass>After that, he went to trial again, got 15 years, bringing his total up to 75. Then he went to trial again, got 60 years. Though in one of those weird legal things that make very little sense to anybody outside of the court system, he serves the 60 at the same time as he serves the 75. Basically, the idea is that if he overturns one of his convictions, someday, on appeal, he still has to serve tons of time.
<v Ira Glass>在那之后，他再次接受审判，得到了15年，使他的总刑期达到75年。然后他又接受审判，被判了60年。尽管在那些让法庭系统外的人难以理解的奇怪法律操作之一中，他可以同时服刑这60年和75年。基本上，想法是如果他将来上诉推翻其中一个定罪，他仍然要服大量的刑期。

00:42:58.620 --> 00:43:09.580
<v Ira Glass>This was an unusually long sentence for armed robbery. So much time that the state simply dropped the other 13 counts against him. Why bother? He'd be away 'til he was an old man.
<v Ira Glass>对于武装抢劫来说，这是一个不同寻常的长刑期。时间太长了，以至于州政府干脆放弃了对他剩下的13项指控。何必费事呢？他要被关到老了。

00:43:09.580 --> 00:43:41.330
<v Steve Bogira>The day the judge gave Vincent the second 30 years, he also sentenced another defendant in his courtroom for murdering two people to 60 years. This defendant had strangled one person with a telephone cord and stabbed the other person 21 times. He got the same 60 years that Vincent had at that point. The difference was the murderer pled guilty.
<v Steve Bogira>法官给文森特第二次30年的那天，他也给法庭里的另一名被告判了60年，那人谋杀了两个人。这名被告用电话线勒死了一个人，并刺了另一个人21刀。他得到了和文森特当时一样的60年。区别在于，那个杀人犯认罪了。

00:43:41.330 --> 00:43:57.530
<v Steve Bogira>Vincent, at the time, had found Jesus in the jail. And I think he felt that Jesus might help him win some of his cases. And I also feel he didn't like the idea of pleading guilty to some crimes which we hadn't done.
<v Steve Bogira>文森特当时在监狱里找到了耶稣。我想他觉得耶稣可能会帮助他赢得一些案件。我也觉得他不喜欢认下一些他没做过的罪名。

00:43:57.530 --> 00:44:00.850
<v Ira Glass>Even though he had done many of those crimes, he hadn't done them all.
<v Ira Glass>即使他确实做了其中的许多罪行，但他并没有全做。

00:44:00.850 --> 00:44:02.540
<v Steve Bogira>Right.
<v Steve Bogira>对。

00:44:02.540 --> 00:44:20.870
<v Ira Glass>Now you've written about this for the Alicia Patterson Reporter. And just a few weeks ago, you went down to Joliet where Vincent is in a maximum security prison. And you talked to him about why he said no to the plea bargains, why he said no to the deal for 25 years.
<v Ira Glass>现在你已经为《艾丽西亚·帕特森报道》（Alicia Patterson Reporter）写过这个故事了。就在几周前，你去了乔利特（Joliet），文森特在那里的一所最高安全级别监狱服刑。你和他谈了为什么拒绝认罪协议，为什么拒绝25年的交易。

00:44:20.870 --> 00:44:21.980
<v Steve Bogira>How old were you at this time?
<v Steve Bogira>你当时多大？

00:44:21.980 --> 00:44:24.390
<v Vincent Bogan>At the time, I was 21.
<v Vincent Bogan>当时，我21岁。

00:44:24.390 --> 00:44:26.650
<v Steve Bogira>So what was going through your head?
<v Steve Bogira>所以你当时在想什么？

00:44:26.650 --> 00:44:41.030
<v Vincent Bogan>It was so many things going through my head, I can't really-- as far as the sentence was concerned, I was looking at 25 years. Half of that time would be 12 and 1/2 years.
<v Vincent Bogan>我脑子里想的事情太多了，我真的不能——就刑期而言，我看到的是25年。那时间的一半就是12年半。

00:44:41.030 --> 00:44:45.220
<v Steve Bogira>With day for day good time, 25 means you serve 12 and 1/2.
<v Steve Bogira>如果考虑到表现良好的减刑，25年意味着你要服刑12年半。

00:44:45.220 --> 00:45:19.710
<v Vincent Bogan>Exactly. And I couldn't really see 12 and 1/2 years of my life going by without me putting up a fight. I'm fighting for my life, what I felt at that particular time. I was 21 years old. I couldn't remember back 12 and 1/2 years.
<v Vincent Bogan>确切地说。我真的无法想象在不经过抗争的情况下，我的生命就那样流逝12年半。我在为我的生命而战，那是当时我的真实感受。我才21岁。我甚至记不起12年半以前的事情。

00:45:19.710 --> 00:45:50.520
<v Vincent Bogan>I believe that the Lord had touched my heart and was directing me not to take this cop-out. And that was a strong conviction on my part. My family didn't understand. My attorney didn't understand. But I felt like that's the way I was being led at that time. And I still feel like that was the way that the Lord had led me to go as far as not copping out to these crimes.
<v Vincent Bogan>我相信上帝触动了我的心，指引我不要接受这个交易。这是我内心强烈的信念。我的家人不理解。我的律师不理解。但我感到那是当时我被引领的方向。而且我依然觉得，上帝在拒绝认罪这方面指引我走那条路。

00:45:50.520 --> 00:45:54.500
<v Steve Bogira>Why did you think that? You were guilty of some of these armed robberies, right?
<v Steve Bogira>为什么你会那么想？你当时确实犯了其中一些武装抢劫罪，对吧？

00:45:54.500 --> 00:45:55.260
<v Vincent Bogan>Right.
<v Vincent Bogan>对。

00:45:55.260 --> 00:45:59.290
<v Steve Bogira>So why do you think that Jesus wanted you to go to trial?
<v Steve Bogira>那你为什么觉得耶稣想让你接受庭审？

00:45:59.290 --> 00:46:19.860
<v Vincent Bogan>Well, I don't think it was so much that he wanted me to go to trial. But I felt secure in whatever I did. Whatever decision I made, I felt that the Lord was going to be with me.
<v Vincent Bogan>嗯，我不觉得那是他想让我接受庭审。但我感觉无论我做什么都感到安心。无论我做出什么决定，我都感到上帝会与我同在。

00:46:19.860 --> 00:47:03.290
<v Vincent Bogan>And I don't believe that the Lord has put me here to leave me here. But I know this. That I learned a serious lesson, a serious life lesson about being in a maximum-security penitentiary. If I woulda took the cop-out, I woulda went straight to a medium-security penitentiary. I never woulda seen behind the walls of these type of penitentiaries. And I believe that, in my particular case, I needed that. I needed to know the crimes I did can call for this particular punishment and for a long period of time.
<v Vincent Bogan>而且我不相信上帝把我放在这里是为了让我一直待在这里。但我知道一件事，我在一所最高安全级别的监狱里学到了一个严肃的教训，一个深刻的人生教训。如果我接受了那个交易，我会直接去一所中等安全级别的监狱。我永远不会见到这类监狱围墙后的景象。我相信，就我的情况而言，我需要那个。我需要知道我所犯下的罪行可能需要这种特定的惩罚以及长期的时间。

00:47:03.290 --> 00:47:06.820
<v Ira Glass>Now in the courts here in Illinois, there's something called "the tax?"
<v Ira Glass>现在在伊利诺伊州的法院，有什么叫“税”吗？

00:47:06.820 --> 00:47:59.730
<v Steve Bogira>Well, it's not an official term, but it's one that everyone down at 26th Street in Cook County at the courthouse recognizes. And what it is is a penalty for asserting your right to trial. In other words, if you're offered a certain number of years, if you decide to take a trial and you get found guilty, most likely, you're going to get more time than you would have if you had pled. Seems to make sense on the surface at least. Because if you didn't have that kind of an incentive, everybody would be going to trial, taking a chance on getting found innocent. And with a huge number of cases to deal with, the courts could not possibly deal with that.
<v Steve Bogira>嗯，这不是一个官方术语，但它是库克县第26街法院里每个人都认可的。它实际上是对行使庭审权利的惩罚。换句话说，如果给你提供了某个年限的刑期，如果你决定接受庭审而被判有罪，很可能你会得到比认罪时更多的刑期。至少表面上看是有道理的。因为如果你没有那种激励机制，每个人都会选择庭审，赌一把自己被判无罪。由于有大量的案件需要处理，法院根本无法应付。

00:47:59.730 --> 00:48:02.850
<v Ira Glass>They couldn't deal with everybody going to trial. They need people to take the plea.
<v Ira Glass>他们无法处理每个人都接受庭审的情况。他们需要人们接受辩诉交易。

00:48:02.850 --> 00:48:35.110
<v Steve Bogira>Well, last year in Cook County, just under 40,000 cases were disposed of by jury, by bench, and by plea. About 33,000 of those were disposed of by plea. I might add that the tax is usually greater if you insist on a jury trial instead of just a bench trial. In a bench trial, the judge decides whether you're guilty or innocent. A jury trial takes a lot longer because you have to pick a jury, you have to instruct the jury, and you have to make sure that certain evidence does not appear before the jury.
<v Steve Bogira>嗯，去年在库克县，有近4万起案件通过陪审团、法官审理和认罪来结案。其中大约3.3万起是通过认罪解决的。我必须补充说，如果你坚持陪审团审判而不是法官独审，这个“税”通常会更高。在法官独审中，法官决定你是否有罪。陪审团审判花费的时间要长得多，因为你必须挑选陪审团，必须指示陪审团，并且必须确保某些证据不会出现在陪审团面前。

00:48:35.110 --> 00:49:11.880
<v Steve Bogira>So jury trials-- they're a nuisance for the system. They take up a lot of time. So if you go to trial and you insist on a jury trial, you're likely to get a little bit more time if you're convicted than if you just had a bench trial. So last year, as I said, 33,000 pleas. Of the other 7,000 cases, there were about 6,300 bench trials. And I believe there were just under 400 jury trials, less than 1% of the cases that were disposed of in Cook County were disposed of by a jury.
<v Steve Bogira>所以陪审团审判——它们对系统来说是个麻烦。它们占用大量时间。所以如果你去庭审并坚持陪审团审判，如果你被定罪，你很可能会比法官独审得到更多的时间。所以去年，就像我说的，3.3万起认罪。在剩下的7000起案件中，大约有6300起法官独审。而且我相信只有不到400起陪审团审判，库克县结案的案件中只有不到1%是通过陪审团审理的。

00:49:11.880 --> 00:49:15.240
<v Ira Glass>And is this necessarily a bad thing?
<v Ira Glass>这一定是件坏事吗？

00:49:15.240 --> 00:50:09.400
<v Steve Bogira>Well, I wouldn't say it's necessarily a bad thing. It depends on your philosophy. If you feel that the criminal justice system is a healthy way to deal with behavior that we don't condone, you'd probably be in favor of a tax. Because it allows the criminal justice system to work and to work even if it has rising caseloads. If you feel that society maybe should look for other ways to dissuade people from participating in activity that we don't condone, then you probably think that we should do the radical thing and abide by the Constitution, especially the Sixth Amendment, which guarantees everybody a right to a jury trial. And that's a right that's not supposed to be fettered by a tax or a punishment if you assert that right.
<v Steve Bogira>嗯，我不会说这一定是件坏事。这取决于你的哲学观。如果你认为刑事司法系统是处理我们不能容忍的行为的一种健康方式，你可能会支持这种“税”。因为它允许司法系统运作，即使在案件量激增的情况下也能运作。如果你认为社会也许应该寻找其他方式来劝阻人们参与我们不能容忍的活动，那么你可能会认为我们应该做些激进的事情，去遵守宪法，特别是第六修正案，它保证每个人都有权接受陪审团审判。那是一项不应该被行使该权利时的税或惩罚所束缚的权利。

00:50:09.400 --> 00:50:13.340
<v Ira Glass>And in Vincent's case, did he ask for a jury trial? Or did he get a bench trial?
<v Ira Glass>在文森特的情况下，他要求陪审团审判了吗？还是法官独审？

00:50:13.340 --> 00:50:14.790
<v Steve Bogira>Jury trials.
<v Steve Bogira>陪审团审判。

00:50:14.790 --> 00:50:16.220
<v Ira Glass>So all of these were jury trials?
<v Ira Glass>所以这些全都是陪审团审判？

00:50:16.220 --> 00:50:22.980
<v Steve Bogira>Right. He was a particular nuisance for the courts. His cases were taking up an awful lot of time.
<v Steve Bogira>对。他是法庭的一个大麻烦。他的案子占用了太多时间。

00:50:22.980 --> 00:50:24.730
<v Ira Glass>How many days are we talking about it?
<v Ira Glass>我们要讨论的是多少天？

00:50:24.730 --> 00:50:55.110
<v Steve Bogira>Well, it's not that many days. It's probably a day to pick the jury and a day or two of trial. But the judge in that courtroom could have disposed of many cases in the time that he had to pick juries for Vincent's cases. In that courtroom afterward, some of the regular lawyers, the lawyers assigned to the courtroom, talked about some of the prosecutors would talk to the public defenders and warn them that "You don't want your client to get Boganized, do you?"
<v Steve Bogira>嗯，其实没那么多天。可能是一天挑选陪审团，一两天庭审。但在文森特的案子中，那个法庭的法官本可以处理很多其他案件。在那个法庭之后，一些常规律师，指派给法庭的律师谈论道，有些检察官会警告公设辩护人：“你不想让你的客户被‘博根化’（Boganized）吧？”

00:50:55.110 --> 00:50:56.770
<v Ira Glass>"Boganized," after Vince Bogan.
<v Ira Glass>“博根化”，来自文斯·博根。

00:50:56.770 --> 00:51:03.190
<v Steve Bogira>That's right. Vincent was a poster boy for what happens if you assert your right to trial.
<v Steve Bogira>没错。文森特成了行使庭审权利后果的典型案例。

00:51:03.190 --> 00:51:27.940
<v Vincent Bogan>When I really look back at, OK, you offered me 25 years. Vince, you shoulda took it. Then I say, OK, if I woulda took it, I don't know. God may have had even a better blessing in store for me to take that 25 years.
<v Vincent Bogan>当我真正回首时，好吧，你给了我25年。文斯，你应该接受它的。然后我说，好吧，如果我当时接受了，我不知道。上帝也许为我接受那25年准备了更好的祝福。

00:51:27.940 --> 00:51:44.920
<v Vincent Bogan>I may have went down to a medium-security penitentiary. I may have been able to continue to do the things I'm doing here in this penitentiary. My faith may have been even stronger in the Lord from that point. But I don't know. I don't know.
<v Vincent Bogan>我可能去了中等安全级别的监狱。我可能能够继续做我在这个监狱里正在做的事情。那时我对上帝的信仰可能更坚定。但我不知道。我不知道。

00:51:44.920 --> 00:51:56.990
<v Vincent Bogan>But I know that God know. And I know that all things work together for the good of those who love the Lord and called according to his purpose. And that's how I was looking at this situation.
<v Vincent Bogan>但我知道上帝知道。我知道万事互相效力，叫爱上帝的人得益处，就是按他旨意被召的人。这就是我看待这种情况的方式。

00:51:56.990 --> 00:52:10.380
<v Ira Glass>When Vincent talks about how perhaps God had him refuse the plea, it's like he's throwing himself on to a kind of a pure fatalism. Whatever will happen, will happen.
<v Ira Glass>当文森特谈到上帝如何引导他拒绝认罪时，就像他陷入了一种纯粹的宿命论。无论发生什么，都会发生。

00:52:10.380 --> 00:53:06.680
<v Steve Bogira>Well, I think that's right. And I think the same kind of fatalism was evident in the run Vincent had been on, the armed robberies he was pulling. I'm convinced that he wanted to get caught. He did get caught the last day after pulling three armed robberies in a row several blocks apart in the afternoon. He wasn't wearing a mask or anything. It was not intelligent work as an armed robber if that's what he was trying to do. But I think that there was an element in Vincent that wanted to be caught. Without doing much psychologizing, I'm certain that the deaths of his mom and dad played a role in that. Exactly how it led him to be doing what he was doing, I'm not certain.
<v Steve Bogira>嗯，我想那是对的。而且我认为同样的宿命论在他当时所做的事情、他进行的武装抢劫中也是显而易见的。我深信他想被抓到。在下午连续抢劫三起案件且相隔几个街区后，他确实在最后一天被抓到了。他没有戴口罩什么的。如果他真的想当一个成功的武装劫匪，那不是明智的行为。但我认为文森特内心有一种想被抓到的元素。不需要做太多心理分析，我肯定他父母的死亡在其中起了作用。至于它究竟如何导致他做他当时做的事情，我不确定。

00:53:06.680 --> 00:53:11.780
<v Ira Glass>In all of your talks with him, did he ever say that he felt like he wanted to be caught? Did he ever admit to that?
<v Ira Glass>在你所有与他的谈话中，他有没有说过他感觉想被抓？他有没有承认过？

00:53:11.780 --> 00:53:18.350
<v Steve Bogira>No, I've asked him about that. He said it was a relief when he was caught.
<v Steve Bogira>没有，我问过他。他说被抓是一种解脱。

00:53:18.350 --> 00:53:39.100
<v Vincent Bogan>I thank God for the prosecutors and judges. Romans 13 says, "Submit yourself to those who have authority over you." I believe that. If I'm wrong, that wrong should be corrected.
<v Vincent Bogan>我感谢上帝赐予检察官和法官。罗马书13章说：“顺服掌权者。”我相信这一点。如果我错了，那个错误应该被纠正。

00:53:39.100 --> 00:54:37.320
<v Ira Glass>Just to get back to the way that he explains to himself what happened, if you think about somebody who made one decision at 21, who said, no, at 21 once essentially-- or in this case, four times in rapid succession-- and then has to live with that for the next 39 years, now that he's had a decade to think about it, it seems like there are only two ways that a person could think about it. One is, I made a terrible mistake and just be racked by that. Or the other, there was a reason, there must be a reason. In this case, God has a reason, and I will just accept that and move forward. And in a way, it's so much healthier or more life preserving for him to believe that there must be a reason that this worked out this way.
<v Ira Glass>回到他解释发生在他身上事情的方式，如果你考虑一个在21岁做出一个决定的人，在21岁时本质上一次——或者在这个案例中，连续四次——说“不”，然后不得不带着那个决定生活接下来的39年。既然他已经思考了十年，似乎一个人只能有两种思考方式。一种是，我犯了一个可怕的错误，然后为此而受折磨。另一种是，有原因，一定有原因。在这个案例中，上帝有原因，我只需接受并向前看。从某种意义上说，相信一切必有原因，对他来说更健康，也更能保全自己。

00:54:37.320 --> 00:55:08.490
<v Steve Bogira>Yeah, I think that Vincent is a remarkably healthy person now. And by healthy, I mean he's really adapted to his destiny. That doesn't mean that this is a success story by any means. I think it's tragic and it's mind boggling to think about someone spending the next three decades in prison who really doesn't need to be there.
<v Steve Bogira>是的，我认为文森特现在是一个非常健康的人。我所说的健康是指他真正适应了自己的命运。这绝不意味着这是一个成功的故事。我认为这是悲剧性的，想到一个人要在监狱里度过未来三十年，而他其实不需要待在那里，真是令人难以置信。

00:55:08.490 --> 00:55:40.520
<v Steve Bogira>Vincent went into prison and very quickly started to do positive things. He's never been in any discipline problems at all in the prison since he's been incarcerated. He's worked as a photographer and the editor of The Pen News, the pentitentiary news, when he was in Pontiac. He worked jobs. He went to school. He's a painter. He does oil paintings mostly of classic cars, and he's very good at it.
<v Steve Bogira>文森特入狱后很快就开始做积极的事情。自从被监禁以来，他从未在监狱里出现过纪律问题。当他在庞蒂亚克（Pontiac）监狱时，他担任过摄影师和《笔尖新闻》（The Pen News）的编辑。他勤恳工作。他上学。他是个画家。他画油画，主要是老爷车，画得非常好。

00:55:40.520 --> 00:55:53.610
<v Steve Bogira>Vincent was back on the right track within probably a year or two. And I don't think it was because he had a 75-year sentence hanging over his head.
<v Steve Bogira>文森特可能在入狱一两年内就回到了正轨。我不认为这是因为他头顶悬着75年的刑期。

00:55:53.610 --> 00:55:58.090
<v Ira Glass>If he had just 12 and 1/2 years ahead of him, probably the same thing, you're saying.
<v Ira Glass>如果他面前只有12年半，你是在说可能结果也是一样。

00:55:58.090 --> 00:56:20.510
<v Steve Bogira>It may have been regardless of what he had hanging over his head. He needed some time out. And most guys don't do positive time like Vincent has. There's no doubt in my mind that Vincent could have come out a few years ago and gotten a job and been a productive citizen.
<v Steve Bogira>无论悬在他头上的是什么，可能结果都一样。他需要一点时间。大多数人不会像文森特那样积极地度过服刑时光。毫无疑问，文森特几年前本可以出来，找到工作并成为一名多产的公民。

00:56:20.510 --> 00:56:43.610
<v Vincent Bogan>I was stubborn when they offered me that time really out of ignorance. I don't know why I didn't see the need to cop out for that 25 years. But I didn't. Now if I was offered that cop-out today, of course I'd take it.
<v Vincent Bogan>当时他们给我那些时间时，我确实是因为无知而固执。我不知道为什么我当时没看出接受那25年交易的必要性。但我没看出来。现在如果今天给我那个交易，我当然会接受。

00:56:43.610 --> 00:56:46.360
<v Steve Bogira>Even if it meant pleading guilty to 17?
<v Steve Bogira>即使这意味着承认17项罪名？

00:56:46.360 --> 00:56:59.340
<v Vincent Bogan>Of course. Even though that can never happen.
<v Vincent Bogan>当然。尽管那永远不会发生了。

00:56:59.340 --> 00:57:23.180
<v Ira Glass>Steve Bogira is working on a book about a year in a courtroom here in Chicago. Vince Bogan's 75-year sentence will probably last 37 and 1/2 years because of good behavior. He'll get out around the year 2025, nearly 60 years old. If he hadn't said no to his plea bargain, he'd be out next year.
<v Ira Glass>史蒂夫·博吉拉正在写一本关于在芝加哥法庭度过一年的书。由于表现良好，文斯·博根的75年刑期可能会持续37年半。他将在2025年左右出狱，接近60岁。如果他当时没对认罪交易说“不”，他明年就能出狱了。

00:57:23.180 --> 00:57:39.530
<v Ira Glass>Well, our program was produced by Susan Burton and myself with Alex Blumberg, Julie Snyder, and Nancy Updike. Contributing editors Paul Tough, Jack Hitt, Margy Rochlin, and Consigliere Sarah Vowell. Production help from Starlee Kine, Todd Bachmann, Sylvia Lemus, and Jorge Just, who ends his internship with our program today and who we wish the very best for.
<v Ira Glass>好了，我们的节目由苏珊·伯顿（Susan Burton）和我制作，亚历克斯·布隆伯格（Alex Blumberg）、朱莉·斯奈德（Julie Snyder）和南希·厄普代克（Nancy Updike）协助。编辑包括保罗·塔夫（Paul Tough）、杰克·希特（Jack Hitt）、玛吉·罗克林（Margy Rochlin）以及顾问莎拉·沃威尔（Sarah Vowell）。制作帮助来自斯塔莉·凯恩（Starlee Kine）、托德·巴赫曼（Todd Bachmann）、西尔维娅·勒姆斯（Sylvia Lemus）和豪尔赫·贾斯特（Jorge Just），他今天结束了在我们节目的实习，我们祝他一切顺利。

00:57:39.530 --> 00:57:43.230
<v Ira Glass>[ACKNOWLEDGEMENTS]
<v Ira Glass>[致谢]

00:57:43.230 --> 00:58:02.780
<v Ira Glass>To buy a cassette of this program, call us here at WBEZ in Chicago, 312-832-3380. Or you know, you can listen to most of our programs for free on the internet at our website, www.thislife.org. Thanks to Elizabeth Meister who runs the site. This American Life is distributed by Public Radio International.
<v Ira Glass>欲购买此节目的磁带，请致电我们在芝加哥的WBEZ电台，电话312-832-3380。或者你知道，你可以通过我们的网站www.thislife.org在互联网上免费收听我们的大部分节目。感谢运营该网站的伊丽莎白·迈斯特（Elizabeth Meister）。《美国生活》由国际公共广播电台（PRI）发行。

00:58:02.780 --> 00:58:36.330
<v Ira Glass>[FUNDING CREDITS]
<v Ira Glass>[资助名单]

00:58:36.330 --> 00:58:40.870
<v Ira Glass>WBEZ management oversight by Torey Malatia, who struggles valiantly every day--
<v Ira Glass>WBEZ管理层监督由托里·马拉蒂亚（Torey Malatia）负责，他每天都在英勇地奋斗——

00:58:40.870 --> 00:58:45.580
<v Geoff Dyer>I've devoted more of my life to thoughts of giving up than anyone else I can think of.
<v Geoff Dyer>我一生中在“放弃”这个念头上花费的时间，比我能想到的任何人都多。

00:58:45.580 --> 00:58:56.140
<v Ira Glass>I'm Ira Glass. Back next week with more stories of This American Life.
<v Ira Glass>我是艾拉·格拉斯。下周带回《美国生活》的更多故事。

00:58:56.140 --> 00:59:01.140
<v Announcer>PRI, Public Radio International.
<v Announcer>PRI，国际公共广播电台。
